@flexiarg argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable
@title Show an Example of the Deliverable
@keywords deliverable, example, pattern, artefact, concreteness
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the Wikimedia IEG 2013 comments on flop 5 (appendices/05, 'Comments'). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term argument-coherence/one-worked-instance argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  When the proposal's product is a kind of thing the audience may not know, include one example of it and say what it is for.

  + context:
    Authors who work with a form daily (patterns, handbooks, protocols) forget that the audience has never seen one.

  + IF:
    The deliverable is an artefact type unfamiliar to the audience.

  + HOWEVER:
    One example can be mistaken for the whole, or argued with in detail.

  + THEN:
    Include one short, real example of the deliverable, labelled as an example, and a sentence on how it is used.

  + BECAUSE:
    Flop 5 proposed a pattern language for Wikimedia. Reviewer: 'Can you provide some examples of patterns, and briefly describe what a pattern is and what it's useful for?'

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
